  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening regional architecture</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">As a founding member and longstanding partner of the Pacific Islands Forum Fisheries Agency (FFA) and the Pacific Community (SPC), Australia contributes approximately 16% of FFA&#x2019;s budget plus additional in-kind support and approx. 11% of SPC Fisheries Aquaculture and Marine Ecosystems Division budget. A 2025 evaluation assessed that Australia&apos;s support to both SPC and FFA over 15 years has strengthened the capacity of these organisations to assist Pacific island countries manage, develop and benefit from healthy fisheries. The Western and Central Pacific is now the only international tuna fishery where all target stocks are sustainable. The Pacific region has benefited from increased value and government revenue from the tuna fishery and licencing fees, increased employment in the tuna fisheries sector, an estimated 37% reduction in illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing from strengthened regional cooperation, and implementation of community-based management for healthy coastal marine resources. The 2025 evaluation highlighted that Australia&#x2019;s flexible and untagged funding has enabled regional organisations to leverage financial contributions from other donors and partners, which amplifies the impact of Australia&#x2019;s contribution.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported 473 communities across Vanuatu, Kiribati and Solomon Islands to develop and implement community management frameworks that support long term sustainability of coastal fisheries resources. Under these frameworks communities have established closed (tabu) areas that they closely monitor. This practice helps to protect resources so that communities can access fish and other marine species to meet protein and food security needs in times of hardship. Most communities that implemented community-based approaches in 2023 reported either improved or consistent health of marine resources and reefs that supported their livelihoods.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s support for Community Based Fisheries Management (CBFM) programs in the Pacific enhance food security, improve nutrition and diversify livelihoods ($6m, 2020-2024 + $2m ACIAR co-funding). In 2022-23 there was strong progress in scaling up CBFM in Kiribati, Solomon Islands and Vanuatu. Kiribati formally recognised the first &#x2018;urban-bordering&#x2019; marine protected area in legislation, Solomon Islands developed a national strategy to increase capability of sub-national fisheries authorities to support CBFM and Vanuatu completed a national strategy to scale up CBFM.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Coastal resources are critical for food security of Pacific communities. Accurate information about coastal catches helps to prevent resource depletion, however manual data collection is cumbersome and erroneous. Australia supported the Pacific Community to develop a cutting-edge Artificial Intelligence-assisted application called Ikasavea (means fish survey). Communities use Ikasavea on their mobile phones to quickly and accurately identify and record information about coastal species caught. Australia supported Kiribati and Vanuatu to start using Ikasavea in 2023. These communities are now making better management decisions to protect their coastal resources based on near-real time and high-quality data.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
